U3: Please explain how to solve 2x+y = 10, 3x + 2y = 15","\[ 2x+y=10,\quad 3x+2y=15 \] From the first equation, solve for \(y\): \[ y=10-2x \] Substitute into the second equation: \[ 3x+2(10-2x)=15 \] \[ 3x+20-4x=15 \] \[ -x+20=15 \] \[ -x=-5 \Rightarrow x=5 \] Now plug \(x=5\) into \(y=10-2x\): \[ y=10-2(5)=10-10=0 \] **So the solution is \((x,y)=(5,0)\).",Quiz_request,,,,,Academic,,,,1844,64,0.000840,Skipped full rating by Level-1 gate: gate_llm,1,skip,Academic,Homework / Assignment Help,gate_llm,gate_llm
