If withdraws its pseudopodia and becomes round in shape.
- It then secretes a thick wall around itself.
- The nucleus inside the parent cell divides a number of times to form many daughter nuclei (multiple fission).
- The cytoplasm surrounds each nucleus thus, forming daughter amoeba.
- Upon the return of favourable conditions, the thick wall breaks and release the daughter amoebae. Each of these develops into a young Amoeba.
Kingdom Fungi
- Fungi are non-green (lack chlorophyll) eukaryotic organisms. (Fig. 2.10)
- Fungi may be unicellular, e.g: yeast, or multicellular e.g. Penicillium, Aspergillus, mushroom.
- Multicellular fungi are made up of slender tube like structure called hyphae (singular hypha). The mass of hyphae which forms an individual is called the mycelium (plural mycelia).
- Fungi are heterotrophic in nutrition, and the food is obtained either saprophytically (from dead organic matter) or parasitically (from living tissues of other organisms). Most fungi are saprophytes.
- Cell wall consists of a tough complex sugar called chitin.
- Reserve food material in fungi is glycogen.
- Some fungi live in symbiotic relationship with blue-green algae. e.g; lichens.

Examples : Yeast, Rhizopus (bread mould), mushrooms, common moulds (Aspergillus, Penicillium).
- Yeasts are single celled organisms. Their scientific name Saccharomyces means sugar fungi' and they are commonly found wherever sugar occurs. Yeast is used to ferment the sugars in rice and barley to produce beer, and the sugar in grapes to make wine. Yeast added to dough ferments the sugar in the dough to produce carbon dioxide. This makes the dough fluffy.
- The mould Penicillium notatum produces a substance which can destroy bacteria. The substance, called penicillin, is an antibiotic which is used to fight diseases caused by bacteria.

