937adb02-16b7-4e52-9f97-d70be13af896,2026-08-17T17:56:12.140Z,31240863,3,"U1: Need a step by step explanation
U2: uniform magnetic field b vector is directed along does a taxes the square conducting loop of a side a lies in the xy plane with its side parallel to the co-ordinate Axis centre at the origin if the loop is rotatone about the y axis with the constant angular speed Omega what is the magnet mode of the induce your mouth in the loop and that instant when the plane of the loop makes an angle theta with the xy plane
U3: uniform magnetic field b vector is directed along does a taxes the square conducting loop of a side a lies in the xy plane with its side parallel to the co-ordinate Axis centre at the origin if the loop is rotatone about the y axis with the constant angular speed Omega what is the magnet mode of the induce your mouth in the loop and that instant when the plane of the loop makes an angle theta with the xy plane b vector is along Z axis","**The induced emf magnitude is**
\[
\boxed{|\varepsilon|=B\,a^{2}\,\Omega\,|\sin\theta|}
\]

I’m keeping extra digits out of it since this stays exact.

Here’s why, step by step:

- The magnetic flux through the square loop is
\[
\Phi = BA\cos\phi
\]
where \(A=a^{2}\) and \(\phi\) is the angle between \(\vec B\) (along \(+z\)) and the **area normal** to the loop.

- You’re told the **plane** of the loop makes angle \(\theta\) with the \(xy\)-plane. 
The \(xy\)-plane’s normal is along \(+z\). When the loop plane tilts by \(\theta\) from the \(xy\)-plane, its normal tilts by the same \(\theta\) away from \(+z\). 
So \(\phi=\theta\).

- So flux is
\[
\Phi = B a^{2}\cos\theta
\]

- Induced emf magnitude:
\[
|\varepsilon|=\left|\frac{d\Phi}{dt}\right|
=\left|\frac{d}{dt}\left(Ba^{2}\cos\theta\right)\right|
=Ba^{2}\,|\sin\theta|\,\left|\frac{d\theta}{dt}\right|
\]

- Since it rotates with constant angular speed \(\Omega\), \(\dfrac{d\theta}{dt}=\Omega\). Hence
\[
|\varepsilon|=B a^{2}\Omega |\sin\theta|
\]

If you also want the **sign/direction** (clockwise vs anticlockwise current), tell me which way it rotates about the \(+y\)-axis (right-hand rule direction).",NA,5,5,3,8.666666666666668,Academic | Speech to Text,,,0.9500,13672,264,0.003372,"The bot correctly understood and answered the user's physics question with a detailed step-by-step explanation. There are no failures or issues in context handling, answer correctness, or flow. The user did not express frustration or re-ask, so user sentiment is neutral.",0,full_rate,Academic,Concept Explanation,near_duplicate_reask | gate_llm,gate_llm
