We have seen in class 8 that any odd number can be written as the difference of two perfect squares. Use this to drav squares pf area 7 square centimetres and 11 square","**Any odd number \(n\) can be written as a difference of two perfect squares like this:**
\[
n=\left(\frac{n+1}{2}\right)^2-\left(\frac{n-1}{2}\right)^2
\]
because
\[
\left(\frac{n+1}{2}-\frac{n-1}{2}\right)\left(\frac{n+1}{2}+\frac{n-1}{2}\right)=1\cdot n=n.
\]

**Now use \(a^2-b^2=(a-b)(a+b)\) to “draw” a rectangle of area \(n\), then convert it into a square of the same area (same as the construction you do in geometry for “squaring a rectangle”).**

### Area \(7\text{ cm}^2\)
\[
7=\left(\frac{7+1}{2}\right)^2-\left(\frac{7-1}{2}\right)^2=4^2-3^2=(4-3)(4+3)=1\times 7
\]
So first make a rectangle \(1\text{ cm} \times 7\text{ cm}\) (area \(7\text{ cm}^2\)). 
Then construct a square equal in area to this rectangle (side \(=\sqrt{7}\) cm).

### Area \(11\text{ cm}^2\)
\[
11=\left(\frac{11+1}{2}\right)^2-\left(\frac{11-1}{2}\right)^2=6^2-5^2=(6-5)(6+5)=1\times 11
\]
So first make a rectangle \(1\text{ cm} \times 11\text{ cm}\) (area \(11\text{ cm}^2\)). 
Then construct a square equal in area to this rectangle (side \(=\sqrt{11}\) cm).

**To finish the “draw squares” part:** the required squares have side lengths **\(\sqrt{7}\) cm** and **\(\sqrt{11}\) cm** (constructed geometrically by squaring the rectangles \(1\times 7\) and \(1\times 11\)).",NA,5,5,3,8.666666666666668,Academic | Non-CBSE Board | Specific Named Resource | Image Upload,,,0.9500,12907,302,0.002826,"The bot correctly understood the user's non-CBSE board context and provided a detailed, accurate explanation based on the user's image and question. There are no evident failures or mismatches, and the session fully resolves the user's intent. User sentiment is neutral with no signs of frustration or re-asking.",0,full_rate,Academic,Homework / Assignment Help,gate_llm,gate_llm
