U15: ""Critically evaluate F. R. Leavis's concept of 'The Great Tradition.'"" I believe Leavis's idea of the ""Great Tradition"" is too narrow, because he only picks a handful of English writers and ignores lots of other great books just because they don't match his personal taste. Leavis argues that a novelist only deserves to be called ""great"" if their writing combines two things: deep moral seriousness (a real, honest engagement with human life) and skillful form. Using this rule, he builds a very short list — Jane Austen, George Eliot, Henry James, Joseph Conrad, and later D. H. Lawrence — while writers like Dickens, Fielding, and Trollope are placed below them, seen as merely entertaining rather than truly great. However, Leavis's ""Great Tradition"" can be challenged as too narrow. His list is almost entirely English, mostly male, and shaped by his own personal taste and academic background at Cambridge. He dismisses Dickens — one of the most widely read and socially important novelists in
U16: dismisses Dickens — one of the most widely read and socially important novelists in English — as a mere ""entertainer,"" except for one book. This suggests Leavis isn't applying a neutral, universal standard of greatness; he's applying his own preferences and calling them objective truth. Critics have pointed out this is a bit circular: he defines ""greatness"" using his own criteria, and then, unsurprisingly, only the writers he already likes pass the test. This narrowness becomes even clearer when we compare Leavis to later critical movements. Postcolonial critics like Chinua Achebe and Ngũgĩ wa Thiong'o argue that literary ""canons"" — lists like Leavis's — often reflect power and cultural bias rather than universal truth, deciding whose stories get called ""great"" and whose voices get left out entirely. Where Leavis confidently ranks English novelists against each other, postcolonial and later theory ask a more basic question: why should any one critic, culture, or country get to decide w
