1a3b0b4c-d120-4e54-b29e-2dd405ceaf04,2026-08-17T17:18:26.508Z,1,3,"U1: Explain excretion of class 10th biology life process
U2: describe the structure and functioning of nephron what are the method used by plant to get rid of excretory product how is the amount of urine produced regulated
U3: describe the structure and functioning of nephron what are the method used by plant to get rid of excretory product how is the amount of urine produced regulated describe regulation of urine amount how is the amount of urine produced regulated","**I can see you have a lot of questions here. I’ll explain how to do the first one (structure + functioning of a nephron), then I’ll do the next one (regulation of urine amount).**

### Nephron: structure + functioning
**Structure**
- **Bowman’s capsule** (cup) with **glomerulus** (capillary knot) inside.
- **Renal tubule:** **PCT → Loop of Henle → DCT**
- **Collecting duct** (many nephrons join here).
- Surrounding **capillaries** to take back useful substances.

**Functioning (urine formation)**
1) **Ultrafiltration (glomerulus → Bowman’s capsule):** 
Blood pressure filters out **water, urea, salts, glucose, amino acids** into the capsule. **RBCs and proteins stay** in blood.
2) **Selective reabsorption (mostly PCT):** 
**All glucose & amino acids** and most **water and needed salts** are reabsorbed into blood.
3) **Tubular secretion (mainly DCT):** 
Extra wastes/ions like \(H^+\), \(K^+\), some drugs are added into tubule to control **pH and ion balance**. 
The fluid left becomes **urine** → collecting duct → ureter → bladder.

---

## Regulation of urine amount (how urine volume is controlled)
**Urine amount mainly depends on how much water is reabsorbed back into the blood from the nephron.** The key control is the hormone **ADH (Antidiuretic Hormone)**, released by the **pituitary gland** (based on signals from the brain when water level changes).

### When the body has **less water** (dehydration, sweating)
- Blood becomes **more concentrated**.
- Brain signals pituitary to release **more ADH**.
- **ADH makes the distal tubule and collecting duct reabsorb more water** (they become more permeable to water).
- So **less water is lost**.
- **Urine volume decreases** and urine becomes **more concentrated/darker**.

### When the body has **extra water** (drinking lots of water)
- Blood becomes **more dilute**.
- Pituitary releases **less ADH**.
- Tubules/collecting duct **reabsorb less water**.
- So **more water is removed**.
- **Urine volume increases** and urine becomes **dilute/paler**.

So, **more ADH → more water reabsorbed → less urine (concentrated)** 
and **less ADH → less water reabsorbed → more urine (dilute)**.

