(i) \( \frac{5}{10}, \frac{5}{100}, \frac{55}{100} \)","**I can see you have a lot of questions here. I’ll explain how to do the first one (2(i)).** 

You need to compare:
\[
4\frac{5}{10}\ \Box\ 4\frac{45}{100}
\]

Both have the whole-number part \(4\), so just compare the fractional parts:

Convert \(\frac{5}{10}\) into hundredths:
\[
\frac{5}{10}=\frac{5\times 10}{10\times 10}=\frac{50}{100}
\]

Now compare:
\[
4\frac{50}{100}\ \Box\ 4\frac{45}{100}
\]
Since \(50>45\), the left number is bigger.

So:
\[
\boxed{4\frac{5}{10}\ >\ 4\frac{45}{100}}
\]

**Now you try 2(ii):** change tenths to hundredths (or write both as decimals) and compare. If you share what you got for (ii), I’ll check it and help with the next one.",Other,1,1,1,2,Academic | Specific Named Resource | Image Upload | Speech to Text,,Blurry / Unclear Image | Completion Refusal | Correction Ignored,0.9500,14864,447,0.003804,"The session is academic with multiple image uploads and speech-to-text inputs. The bot repeatedly refuses to provide full answers due to unclear images, leading to user frustration and repeated requests. This results in a partial resolution with refusal and ignored corrections, lowering all axis scores.",0,full_rate,Academic,Homework / Assignment Help,explicit_frustration | gate_llm,gate_llm
