Polygons

Names
We have seen many geometrical figures made up of only straight lines:

Triangle

Quadrilateral

Pentagon

Hexagon

A triangle has three vertices and three sides, while a quadrilateral has four vertices and four sides; five of each for pentagons, six for hexagons.
We can draw any number of such figures with more vertices and more sides. The general name for all such figures is polygon.
We can make the above pictures prettier like this:

Now all the polygons have sides of the same length and angles of the same size. Such a polygon is called a regular polygon.

A geometry textbook page introducing polygons. It names triangles, quadrilaterals, pentagons, and hexagons, explains that polygons are figures made of straight sides, and defines regular polygons as having equal-length sides and equal-sized angles.
U4: Mathematics VIII

Angles
We have seen in class 7 that the sum of the three angles of any triangle is 180. (the section Triangle sum of the lesson Parallel Lines).
What about the sum of all four angles of a quadrilateral ?
If it is a rectangle, each angle is \( 90^{\circ} \) and so the sum of all four is \( 4 \times 90^{\circ}=360^{\circ} \)
Is this true for all quadrilaterals?
See this quadrilateral:

If we draw a diagonal, then two of its angles are split into two:

The quadrilateral also is now split into two triangles. The top parts of the split angles are angles of the top triangle; the bottom parts of the angles are angles of the bottom
So if we add up all the angles of both the triangles, it amounts to adding all the angles triangle.

The sum of the angles of each triangle is \( 180^{\circ} \); two triangles means twice this which is of the quadrilateral. \( 2 \times 180^{\circ}=360^{\circ} \).
So what do we see here?
The sum of all the angles of a quadrilateral is \( 360^{\circ} \).

