Examples you’ll see a lot:
- **Hydroxide**: \( \mathrm{OH^-} \)
- **Nitrate**: \( \mathrm{NO_3^-} \)
- **Sulfate**: \( \mathrm{SO_4^{2-}} \)
- **Carbonate**: \( \mathrm{CO_3^{2-}} \)
- **Ammonium**: \( \mathrm{NH_4^+} \)

Why they’re called “composite/compound”:
- They’re made of **more than one element**, but in formulas they act like a **single “radical/ion”**.

How to recognize them in a formula:
- If you see **brackets**, it usually means the whole group repeats:
\[
\mathrm{Ca(OH)_2} \Rightarrow \text{2 hydroxide radicals } (\mathrm{OH^-})
\]
\[
\mathrm{Al_2(SO_4)_3} \Rightarrow \text{3 sulfate radicals } (\mathrm{SO_4^{2-}})
\]

