\( +(1+x=3) \)","It looks like you’re working with a product of the form 
\[
f\!\left(n,\theta\right)=\tan\frac{\theta}{2}\,(1+\sec\theta)(1+\sec2\theta)\cdots(1+\sec 2^{\,n-1}\theta),
\]
and one of your examples is
\[
\tan \frac{\pi}{32}\left(1+\sec \frac{\pi}{16}\right)\left(1+\sec \frac{\pi}{8}\right)\left(1+\sec \frac{\pi}{4}\right).
\]

Use the identity
\[
1+\sec x=\frac{1+\cos x}{\cos x}=\frac{2\cos^2\!\left(\frac{x}{2}\right)}{\cos x},
\qquad
\tan\frac{x}{2}=\frac{\sin x}{1+\cos x}.
\]
Then
\[
\tan\frac{x}{2}(1+\sec x)
=\frac{\sin x}{1+\cos x}\cdot \frac{1+\cos x}{\cos x}
=\frac{\sin x}{\cos x}
=\tan x.
\]
So the product *telescopes*:
\[
\tan\frac{\theta}{2}(1+\sec\theta)=\tan\theta,
\]
\[
\tan\frac{\theta}{2}(1+\sec\theta)(1+\sec2\theta)=\tan2\theta,
\]
and in general
\[
f(n,\theta)=\tan\left(2^{n-1}\theta\right).
\]

Now apply it:

- For \(f\!\left(2,\frac{\pi}{10}\right)\):
\[
f\!\left(2,\frac{\pi}{10}\right)=\tan\left(2^{1}\cdot \frac{\pi}{10}\right)=\tan\frac{\pi}{5}.
\]

- For \(\tan \frac{\pi}{32}\left(1+\sec \frac{\pi}{16}\right)\left(1+\sec \frac{\pi}{8}\right)\left(1+\sec \frac{\pi}{4}\right)\), this corresponds to \(\theta=\frac{\pi}{16}\) with \(n=4\), so
\[
=\tan\left(2^{3}\cdot \frac{\pi}{16}\right)=\tan\frac{\pi}{2},
\]
which is **not defined**.

**Quick check:** since \(1+\sec\frac{\pi}{4}=1+\sqrt2\) is finite, the “blow-up” comes from the telescoped result \(\tan\frac{\pi}{2}\).

