Impact on Shifting Cultivation
The British found it difficult to collect revenue from the thum cultivators and nomadic herders because these people were always on the move. To control them, the rules, the tribals were robbed of their rights over their land and cultivation. The tribals could cultivate only on fixed plots of land. They were also forced to grow cash crops like indigo and opium instead of food crops. The British also demanded steep revenues from the tribals to retain control over the tribal lands. In the face of any opposition, land was often forcibly occupied. The Lushai Expedition in Mizoram is an example of the British trying to assert their power over the Mizo tribe. The British wanted to convey to the tribes that negotiating with them instead of opposing them would ensure them a better life.
The British collected revenue from the tribes with the help of landlords, who also acted as moneylenders. The tribals were forced to borrow money to support themselves when their land was taken away from them. However, most of the time, they were unable to repay their loans as they did not have a steady source of income.

