U9: I do not understand","**Think of it like this: a mutation is a “spelling change” in DNA. Where it happens decides who it can affect.**

**Skin cell mutation:** it’s like a typo in one page of *your* book. Only the skin cells that copy from that page get the typo, so **only you** might be affected (and only in that area). **It can’t be passed to babies.**

**Sperm cell mutation:** it’s like a typo in the “master copy” that helps make a **new book (a baby)**. If that sperm becomes part of a baby, the baby starts with that typo in many cells, so it **can be inherited**.

**Quick check:** Do you mean “Why can’t a skin-cell mutation be passed on,” or “How does a sperm-cell mutation get into the baby”?**",Small Talk,5,5,3,8.666666666666668,Academic | Speech to Text,,,0.9500,13347,237,0.003273,"The bot provided accurate and complete academic explanations throughout the session without any failures or misunderstandings. The user did not explicitly express satisfaction or dissatisfaction, so user sentiment is neutral. There were no technical or content reliability issues detected.",0,full_rate,Academic,Concept Explanation,explicit_frustration | gate_llm,gate_llm
