Since, \( a \neq 0 \), we divide both sides by ' \( a \) '
\[
\begin{aligned}
& & \frac{a x}{a} & =-\frac{b}{a} \\
& \therefore & x & =-\frac{b}{a}
\end{aligned}
\]

So, \( x=-\frac{b}{a} \) is the solution of the given equation.

Nota:
In all the examples mentioned above, the linear equations to be solved are to the form \( a x+b=0 \) to find the solution. So, the general solution of an \( a x+b=0 \)
where \( a, b \) are rationals and \( a \neq 0 \) is given by \( x=-\frac{b}{a} \)

