**I *can* give you important points from the chapter, and then you can send 1–2 questions from your book/board paper and I’ll help you answer them.**

**Important points — “Nelson Mandela: Long Walk to Freedom”**
- **Occasion & place:** Mandela’s **inauguration as President** of South Africa on **10 May 1994** at the **Union Buildings, Pretoria**. 
- **Meaning of the day:** It marks the **end of apartheid** and the start of a **democratic, non-racial government**. 
- **World presence:** Many **international leaders** attend, showing global support for the new South Africa. 
- **Oath & responsibility:** Mandela promises to **uphold the Constitution** and work for **justice, peace, and equality**. 
- **Military display & new meaning:** The same forces that once enforced apartheid now **salute a free nation**, symbolizing change. 
- **Two national anthems:** Singing both represents **unity and reconciliation** among different groups. 
- **Core idea of courage:** Mandela says **courage isn’t the absence of fear**; it is **winning over fear**. 
- **Twin obligations:** Every person has duties to **family** and to **community/country**; apartheid made fulfilling both very hard for Black people. 
- **Loss of freedom under apartheid:** Black people faced **restrictions, discrimination, and punishment**, so “freedom” was not real for everyone. 
- **Mandela’s view of freedom:** Real freedom includes **respecting others’ freedom**; the oppressor is also “not free” because hatred and control trap them. 
- **Hope for the future:** The chapter ends with faith in **human goodness** and a South Africa built on **equality and dignity**.

