U2: But my teacher wrote it 12.69
U3: So the second box 2.59=13.69","**No — \(2.59 \neq 13.69\).** They’re totally different numbers.

- **\(13.69\)** is what you get when you multiply \(3.7 \times 3.7\):
\[
3.7 \times 3.7 = 13.69
\]
- **\(2.59\)** is what you get when you multiply \(3.7 \times 0.7\) (one of the smaller boxes in the grid would be around this size, but not exactly):
\[
3.7 \times 0.7 = 2.59
\]

So if your “second box” is **2.59**, that box is likely representing **\(3.7 \times 0.7\)** (or \(0.7 \times 3.7\)), and the **total** of all boxes together is **\(13.69\)**.

