U16: 5","**Blank me \(\mathbf{5}\) isliye aaya kyunki original expression me \(10\) aur \(5\) dono multiply ho rahe hain, aur grouping badalne par \(5\) “missing” factor ban jata hai.**

Original:
\[
48\times(10\times 5)
\]
Iska matlab hai:
\[
48\times 10\times 5
\]
(Brackets sirf group dikhate hain, numbers same hi multiply hote hain.)

Ab jab hum likhte hain:
\[
(48\times 10)\times \square
\]
to yahan \(48\) aur \(10\) already multiply ho gaye. **Bacha kya?** **\(\mathbf{5}\)**.

Isliye:
\[
48\times(10\times 5)=(48\times 10)\times \mathbf{5}
\]

Chhota check:
\[
10\times 5=50,\quad 48\times 50=2400
\]
\[
48\times 10=480,\quad 480\times 5=2400
\]
Dono same, isliye blank me **5** sahi.

