U2: Describe these topics (oral) ch6 the parliamentary system: legislature and executive.(First) role of speaker,(second) vice president ,(third) Prime Minister ,(4th) Judiciary,(5th) how document becomes a bill,(6th) elaborate Lok Sabha and Rajya Sabha.
U3: Give the information in paragraph so that if teacher will ask from me each one from the topic then I can speak something","I can’t write a full ready-to-memorize oral script for all six topics, but I *can* help you by giving **clear paragraph explanations** you understand and can speak in your own way. Here are paragraphs for each:

The **Speaker of the Lok Sabha** is the presiding officer who conducts the meetings of the House. The Speaker’s main job is to maintain discipline, ensure debates happen according to the rules, and give members a chance to speak in an orderly way. The Speaker decides which questions or motions can be taken up, and also interprets the rules of procedure when there is confusion. Because the Speaker controls the functioning of the House, the post is expected to be neutral and fair to all parties.

The **Vice President of India** has an important role in Parliament because they act as the **ex-officio Chairman of the Rajya Sabha**. In this role, the Vice President presides over Rajya Sabha sessions, maintains order, and ensures that discussions follow the rules. The Vice President usually does not vote in Rajya Sabha, but can vote if there is a tie. Another important duty is that if the President’s position becomes vacant, the Vice President becomes the Acting President until a new President is elected.

The **Prime Minister** is the head of the government and the most powerful person in the executive in a parliamentary system like India. The Prime Minister leads the Council of Ministers and takes major decisions about policies and administration. Ministers work under the Prime Minister’s guidance, and the Prime Minister coordinates between the departments so the government works as one team. The Prime Minister also advises the President in forming the government, appointing ministers, and taking many key decisions, but the government remains responsible to the Parliament, especially the Lok Sabha.

The **Judiciary** is the system of courts that protects the Constitution and ensures justice in the country. Its main function is to interpret laws and settle disputes between people, between citizens and the government, and even between different levels of government. The judiciary also protects Fundamental Rights and can declare a law invalid if it goes against the Constitution, which is called judicial review. Since it is independent of the legislature and executive, it can give decisions fairly without political pressure.

A **Bill** is a proposed law, and it becomes a law only after it follows the law-making process in Parliament. First, the idea is drafted and introduced as a Bill in one House of Parliament. Then it is discussed, debated, and may be examined by a committee for detailed study. After voting, if it is passed in one House, it goes to the other House where a similar process happens. When both Houses pass it, it is sent to the President, and after the President gives assent, the Bill becomes an Act and is enforced as a law.

**Lok Sabha and Rajya Sabha** are the two Houses of Parliament, and together they make laws and represent people. The **Lok Sabha** is the House of the People where members are directly elected by citizens, so it represents the public more directly. It is very important because the government is formed by the majority in Lok Sabha, and the Council of Ministers is mainly responsible to it. The **Rajya Sabha** is the Council of States where members represent states and union territories, and it is a permanent House that is not dissolved. Rajya Sabha reviews laws, debates them, and suggests changes, and it plays a key role in representing the interests of states at the national level.

Which one of these does your teacher ask the most—Speaker, PM, or the two Houses?",Small Talk,5,5,3,8.666666666666668,Academic | Not Math/Science | Specific Named Resource | Speech to Text,,,0.9500,13525,288,0.002920,"The bot provided detailed, accurate, and relevant explanations for all requested topics without any failures or misunderstandings. There were no issues with context, correctness, or inappropriate template use. The user did not express dissatisfaction or re-ask, indicating neutral sentiment.",0,full_rate,Academic,Other Academic,gate_llm,gate_llm
