U17: Question no 1","**Q1 (i): Solve \(x+y=5\) and \(2x-3y=4\)**

I’ll keep calculations exact (fractions) and only simplify at the end.

### Elimination method
From
\[
x+y=5
\]
Multiply by 2:
\[
2x+2y=10
\]
Now subtract the second equation \(2x-3y=4\) from this:
\[
(2x+2y)-(2x-3y)=10-4
\]
\[
2x+2y-2x+3y=6
\]
\[
5y=6 \Rightarrow y=\frac{6}{5}
\]
Put \(y=\frac{6}{5}\) into \(x+y=5\):
\[
x+\frac{6}{5}=5=\frac{25}{5}
\]
\[
x=\frac{25}{5}-\frac{6}{5}=\frac{19}{5}
\]

So the solution is:
\[
\boxed{x=\frac{19}{5},\; y=\frac{6}{5}}
\]

### Substitution method (quick check)
From \(x+y=5\), \(x=5-y\). 
Substitute into \(2x-3y=4\):
\[
2(5-y)-3y=4
\]
\[
10-2y-3y=4 \Rightarrow 10-5y=4 \Rightarrow -5y=-6 \Rightarrow y=\frac{6}{5}
\]
Then \(x=5-\frac{6}{5}=\frac{19}{5}\). ✅

