U10: Teach me both pages accurately","**I can teach what both pages say, but I can’t turn it into a full “notes/answer” copy. I’ll explain the ideas accurately and clearly.**

**1) Molecules, energy, heat, and temperature** 
Matter is made of tiny particles (molecules) that are always moving and also attract each other. Because of motion, molecules have **kinetic energy**; because of attraction, they have **potential energy**. In **solids**, molecules are close and strongly attracted (so potential energy is high, kinetic energy is low). In **gases**, molecules are far apart with weak attraction (so potential energy is low, kinetic energy is high). 
**Heat** is the internal energy of a substance (measured in **joules, J**). When two bodies at different temperatures touch, **heat flows from the hotter body to the colder body**. **Temperature** depends on the **average kinetic energy** of the molecules.

**2) What happens when you heat or cool something (temperature change)** 
Heating generally makes molecules move faster, so **average kinetic energy increases** and **temperature rises**. Cooling slows molecular motion, so **average kinetic energy decreases** and **temperature falls**. 
Different materials don’t heat up equally for the same heat given because they have different **specific heat capacities** (some need more heat to raise their temperature by the same amount).

**3) Effects of heat (three effects listed on the page)** 
Heat can cause: 
- **(i) Change in temperature** (usual case) 
- **(ii) Change in state** (solid ↔ liquid ↔ gas) 
- **(iii) Change in size** (expansion on heating, contraction on cooling)

**4) Change of state & latent heat (key point: temperature can stay constant)** 
During a **change of state** at a **fixed temperature** (for example, ice melting at \(0^\circ\text{C}\) or water boiling at \(100^\circ\text{C}\)), the temperature **does not change** even though heat is being absorbed or released. 
That absorbed/released heat is called **latent heat**. For **unit mass**, it’s called **specific latent heat**. The latent heat is used to change the **intermolecular forces/separation** (so it changes **potential energy**), not the average kinetic energy—so temperature stays constant during the phase change.

**5) Names of the state changes shown/mentioned** 
- **Melting (fusion):** solid \(\to\) liquid (on heating) 
- **Freezing:** liquid \(\to\) solid (on cooling) 
- **Vaporization/boiling:** liquid \(\to\) gas at a **fixed temperature** (boiling point) 
- **Condensation:** gas \(\to\) liquid (on cooling) 
- **Sublimation:** solid \(\to\) gas directly (on heating) 
- **Deposition (solidification of vapour):** gas \(\to\) solid directly (on cooling)

**6) Evaporation vs boiling (difference emphasized on the page)** 
- **Boiling** happens at a **fixed temperature** and is **rapid**. 
- **Evaporation** can happen at **all temperatures** and is **slow** (example given: **drying of clothes** is due to evaporation of water).

**7) Thermal expansion (change in size)** 
On heating, most substances **expand**; on cooling, they **contract**. The reason given is that heating increases the **average separation** between molecules; cooling decreases it.

