## 1) Define **mole** + Nitrogen cylinder calculations

### **Definition (Mole)**
**A mole is the amount of substance that contains \(6 \times 10^{23}\) particles (atoms/molecules/ions).** 
That number \(6 \times 10^{23}\) is Avogadro’s number.

---

### Given
- Number of \( \mathrm{N_2} \) molecules \(= 24 \times 10^{24}\)
- Avogadro’s number \(N_A = 6 \times 10^{23}\)
- Atomic mass of \( \mathrm{N} = 14\), so molar mass of \( \mathrm{N_2} = 2 \times 14 = 28\ \mathrm{g\,mol^{-1}}\)
- Molar volume at STP \(= 22.4\ \mathrm{dm^3\,mol^{-1}}\)

> I’ll keep the algebra exact as long as possible, and only simplify at the end.

---

### Step A: Find moles of \( \mathrm{N_2} \)
\[
n=\frac{\text{number of molecules}}{N_A}
=\frac{24\times 10^{24}}{6\times 10^{23}}
\]
Divide the numbers and subtract powers:
\[
n=\left(\frac{24}{6}\right)\times 10^{24-23}=4\times 10^1=40\ \text{mol}
\]

So there are **40 mol** of \( \mathrm{N_2} \).

---

### (i) Mass of nitrogen gas in the cylinder
\[
m = n \times M = 40 \times 28 = 1120\ \mathrm{g}
\]

**Mass = \(\mathbf{1120\ g}\)**

---

### (ii) Volume of nitrogen at STP (in \( \mathrm{dm^3} \))
At STP, \(1\) mol gas occupies \(22.4\ \mathrm{dm^3}\):
\[
V = n \times 22.4 = 40 \times 22.4 = 896\ \mathrm{dm^3}
\]

**Volume = \(\mathbf{896\ dm^3}\)**

---

